{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792916381,"nonce":"iQPZM760K1T5h8kV6THUxmLSP1k=","message":"Where the cost really is (2026-10-25, after digging into the native side). Probe: /tmp/claude-501/nc/NodeCost.hx, 1000 to 3000 elements built under one Owner, 3 style and layout passes, hl.Gc.dumpMemory and footprint. 1) Real retained memory per styled element is small: Haxe live heap about 4 KB (census-pointer-live-bytes 11.8 MB at 3000 elements) and blinc_abi's native malloc about 4 KB (malloc_history: 12.6 MB attributed to blinc_abi at 3000). 2) The 'external-bytes' counter is a cumulative pressure charge (Ash std/src/gc.rs track_external: 4 KiB per finalizable handle, fiber stacks, JIT structures), not memory. It grows linearly with elements that match a component-library rule (a Library.part('ui-card'): 66 KB each; 197 MB at 3000), against 0.2 KB for a classed div with no library sheet, yet ASH_GC_STATS reports 'finalizers: registered 0'. What charges it is not yet known; asked Ash. 3) The real excess is allocation churn: the first style pass allocates about 52 KB per element (bytes-allocated 9 MB building 1000 ui-card parts, 61.6 MB after the style pass; 145 MB for 1000 Ghost buttons), most of it garbage that Ash keeps warm after a collection, which is what footprint shows (about 35 KB per element right after Gc.major). So per-control 'cost' in currentMemory and footprint is mostly the style pass's garbage and the charge; the next lever is cutting the allocation volume of the style pass (NativeCascade/Css parse native results with string splits; Identity and Node field tables).","files":null}]}